@@ -2,6 +2,58 @@
 
 This project is a sample news application built with Astro, designed to serve as a testing ground for new features in Chrome DevTools.
 
+## Account settings and passkeys
+
+After signing in, readers are sent to `/devtools-times/account/settings` and can
+also reach it through the header. Sign-in verifies the password with the existing
+Times login service and creates a server-side Astro session lasting 24 hours.
+Passwords and upstream tokens are not saved. Settings and account API responses
+are private (`Cache-Control: no-store`); mutations check the request origin and
+derive account ownership from the session.
+
+The passkey section lists provider, custom display name, registration date and
+last-used date. Readers can rename or confirm removal of each passkey. Successful
+list loads and removals notify supported authenticators using
+`PublicKeyCredential.signalAllAcceptedCredentials`, with the server's complete
+remaining list and Base64URL IDs. Nicknames only change the website's label.
+Unsupported browsers and synchronization failures show manual password-manager
+instructions without undoing a successful server change.
+
+### Storage and registration integration
+
+This starter has **no passkey registration or passkey authentication flow**, and
+the existing remote password-login service has no credential management API.
+No sample credentials are created. New accounts therefore show the empty state.
+To manage previously registered credentials, integrate the verified registration
+and authentication backend with `src/server/accounts.ts` before deployment:
+
+- Use the canonical username returned by the login service as the account lookup
+  key. If your identity provider supports aliases, resolve them to the same
+  canonical username before creating a session.
+- During registration, use `updateAccount` to obtain the account's `userId` for
+  WebAuthn's user handle. After server verification, persist the credential in
+  `account.passkeys`: `id` (Base64URL), `publicKey` (Base64URL), `counter`, `name`,
+  `aaguid`, `registeredAt` (epoch milliseconds), and optional `lastUsedAt` (epoch
+  milliseconds). Keep private keys in the authenticator. When migrating existing
+  credentials, preserve their original Base64URL user handle in `account.userId`.
+- Authentication must read credentials from this same repository, reject missing
+  IDs and persist the verified counter and `lastUsedAt`. This makes deletion
+  revoke the credential, rather than merely hide a metadata entry. Do not
+  maintain a separate authentication store unless deletion updates it as well.
+- Set `PASSKEY_RP_ID` to the registration RP ID when it differs from the current
+  hostname. The default is the request hostname. Serve over HTTPS in production.
+
+Account records are atomically written to `.data/accounts` (excluded from Git),
+or `ACCOUNT_DATA_DIR`. Mount this directory on durable storage. The file
+repository serializes updates within one Node process; replace it with a shared
+transactional database for multiple instances. Astro's Node adapter also uses
+filesystem sessions by default; configure a shared session driver when scaling.
+
+Provider hints are a small local subset of the
+[passkey-authenticator-aaguids registry](https://github.com/passkeydeveloper/passkey-authenticator-aaguids).
+Unknown or zero AAGUIDs use a generic key icon. Provider metadata is only a
+display hint, never an authentication or authorization decision.
+
 
 ```sh
 pnpm install && pnpm run start
